<p>
  Anoles cling to leaves, bark and walls with toepads: pads of enlarged scales on
  the undersides of their fingers and toes, each scale covered in millions of
  microscopic hair-like structures. Bigger pads grip better. In a hurricane, grip
  can decide who survives.
</p>
<p>
  In 2017 Hurricanes Irma and Maria swept across the Caribbean, including two
  small islands in the Turks and Caicos, Pine Cay (350 hectares) and Water Cay
  (250 hectares), where Colin Donihue and colleagues had measured the local anole,
  <em>Anolis scriptus</em>, before the storms. Measuring the survivors
  afterward, they found that the lizards still on the islands had larger toepads
  than the population before the hurricanes.
</p>

<h2>Does it last?</h2>
<p>
  A shift in the survivors is one thing. Would it carry into the next generation,
  or be erased by whatever selection came next? Returning in 2019, the team, which
  included James Stroud, focused on young lizards that had most likely hatched after
  the storms, the offspring of hurricane survivors. Their toepads were just as
  large, relative to body size, as their parents' had been, and still larger than
  those of the lizards measured before the storms.
</p>

<h2>From two islands to 188 species</h2>
<p>
  If hurricanes favor bigger toepads every time they hit, then places hit more
  often should have lizards with bigger toepads. Combining 70 years of historical
  hurricane tracks with toepad measurements, the team tested exactly that at two scales:
  across 12 island populations of the brown anole, and across 188 anole species
  throughout the Neotropics. In both, lizards from places struck by more hurricanes
  tended to have proportionately larger toepads, linking a few violent days in a
  single population to patterns spread across an entire group of species.
</p>
<p>
  Hurricanes are expected to grow more severe as the climate warms. Our results
  suggest that extreme events like these may shape the evolution of the
  communities they hit far more than has been appreciated, acting as brief,
  intense bursts of natural selection rather than simply as disasters to be
  recovered from.
</p>
<p>
  The study, led by Colin Donihue with James Stroud among the co-authors, was
  published in <em>PNAS</em> in 2020.
</p>
